.TH CBTEST 1
.SH NAME
cbtest \- regression test for cb(1)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B cbtest
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I Cbtest
loads
.BR /p9/dis/cb.dis 's
in-process test seam,
.BR run(argv,\ input) ,
and checks it against a real, live oracle - built fresh from the
ACTUAL 9front source every run
.RB ( sys/src/cmd/cb/{cb.c,cbtype.c} ,
compiled via host
.B clang
against plan9port's prebuilt
.BR libbio / lib9 ,
through the same
.IR cmd (3)
device
.IR urlencodetest (1)
established), not plan9port's own prebuilt
.B cb
binary - see
.IR cb (1)'s
own BUGS for why that binary is deliberately not trusted as a
reference here.
.PP
Twelve cases across six groups, each comparing
.BR cb.b 's
own output against the built oracle's, byte for byte:
.TP
.I t1simple / t2ifelse
Small hand-written snippets exercising basic reindentation and, with
.BR t2ifelsestrict ,
the
.B -s
flag.
.TP
.I t3edge
A hand-built file
.RB ( EDGE )
covering
.BR switch / case ,
.BR do / while ,
the
.B ::
generator operator, the ternary
.BR ?: ,
string/character-literal escapes, and both C and C++-style comments -
run in default, strict
.RB ( -s ),
and join
.RB ( -j )
modes.
.TP
.I t4flags
Bundled-flag parsing
.RB ( -sj
as one argument) and the
.B -l
width flag in both its bundled
.RI ( -l 40)
and separate
.RI ( -l\ 40)
forms - run against the real
.B cb.c
source rather than
.BR EDGE ,
because
.BR EDGE 's
own lines are all short enough that
.B -l 40
is a no-op on it (checked directly against the oracle: no-flag,
.BR -l40 ,
and
.B -l\ 40
all produce byte-identical output on
.BR EDGE ).
.B Cb.c
itself has lines long enough that
.B -l\ 40
visibly folds them (1,041 output lines unflagged vs 1,284 with
.BR -l\ 40 ,
confirmed against the oracle), so the bundled-vs-separate comparison
actually exercises the parser rather than merely confirming two
no-ops match.
.TP
.I t5realfile
The real
.B cb.c
source itself (1,259 lines, read via
.IR cmd (3)'s
.B cat
rather than
.BR sys->open() ,
since the path is a HOST absolute path and Inferno's own namespace is
not the host filesystem root), in default and strict modes - checked
by hand against the built oracle before this suite existed, alongside
four other real, unrelated files already in this tree's own
checkouts
.RB ( auth/ssh2rsa.c ,
.BR primes.c ,
.BR join.c ,
and
.B tar.c
- 1,588 lines - all byte-identical in default, strict, join, and
.B -l 40
modes), reconfirmed here as a live regression case for
.B cb.c
specifically.
.TP
.I t6stateisolation
Two independent
.B run()
calls must not contaminate each other - the same lesson
.IR pr (1)'s
own test suite established for its
.B Colw
bug. An UNBALANCED snippet (an open brace with no matching close) is
run first to leave
.BR clevidx ,
the
.B ind[]
indent stack, and several
.BR getch()/getnext()
lookahead scalars dirty at EOF in a way well-formed C would return to
zero on its own; a plain
.B EDGE
run follows and is checked against
.BR EDGE 's
own known default-mode oracle output. Verified by an actual negative
control, not assumed: gutting every scalar reset in
.BR cb.b 's
own
.B resetstate()
(keeping only the array reallocations a from-nil first call still
needs) made this case fail alongside 9 of the other 11 cases - and
separately showed that
.B clevidx
leaking BY ITSELF is not independently observable, since
.B resetstate()
reallocates the whole
.B ind[]
array fresh every call regardless of
.BR clevidx 's
own value; it takes the combination of scalars leaking together to
move the output.
.SH SOURCE
.B /p9/cmd/cbtest.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR cb (1)
.SH BUGS
Translating
.BR cb.c 's
own
.B lookup(char *first, char *last)
found a real bug in this port's first translation pass, by testing
real files and diffing output - not by re-reading the code. The C
function is called with pointers into either of two different
buffers depending on call site (the current-token buffer at some
sites, the temp/lookahead buffer at others); the first Limbo pass
hardcoded it to always read the current-token buffer, so every
lookahead-buffer call site silently matched against the wrong data.
The visible symptom was severe: an
.B else
or
.B case
keyword sitting in the lookahead buffer right after a closing
.B }
went unrecognized, collapsing indentation for the rest of any file
containing an
.BR if{}else{} " construct."
Isolated with a minimal reproducer, root-caused with temporary
diagnostic instrumentation, fixed by making the buffer an explicit
parameter, and confirmed with a genuine negative control: reverting
just the affected call sites reproduced 10 of 12 failures (only the
two cases not exercising a lookahead-buffer path still passed); restoring the fix returned the suite to 12 of 12.
.PP
Does not exercise
.IR cb (1)'s
own multi-file command-line invocation (its
.B init()
opens each
.I file
argument in a loop with no state reset between files, matching the C
source's own preserved behavior) - every case here drives
.BR run() 's
own single
.I input
byte array directly, matching
.BR cb.b 's
own test-seam scope.
.PP
Building the oracle depends on a live host
.B clang
and a checked-out 9front source tree at a fixed path; if either is
missing, the whole suite fails loudly with the compiler's own error
text rather than silently skipping the comparison.
